# Response headers. Cloudflare Pages reads this file verbatim; scripts/serve.py
# parses it so the SAME policy is enforced during local development.
#
# Why this file exists rather than <meta> tags: CSP frame-ancestors,
# X-Content-Type-Options, Referrer-Policy and HSTS are only honoured as response
# headers. A CSP that is not served is not a CSP, and one first discovered at
# deploy time is one that gets waived under pressure.
#
# default-src 'none' means every resource class must be named explicitly below.
# Adding a directive here is a security decision — see docs/phase-1-scope.md §4.2.
#
# connect-src carries 'self' as well as the geocoder, and that 'self' is not a
# relaxation. Leaving it out refused three same-origin fetches the site depends on:
# /data/lookup.json, /data/districts.geo.json and /manifest.json. The first two are
# the privacy feature — a ZIP, a neighbourhood or a district number is supposed to
# resolve from a committed index and never reach the geocoder — so without 'self'
# every query fell through to the geocoder, and a policy written to protect the
# reader's address was causing more of their input to be sent, not less. The third is
# the staleness notice, which silently never appeared.
#
# tests/unit/test_csp.py cross-checks this list against every URL the frontend
# fetches, and scripts/console_check.py fails on any policy violation in a real
# browser. Neither existed when this line was first written, which is why it shipped.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self'; img-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https://geosearch.planninglabs.nyc; form-action 'none'; base-uri 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), camera=(), microphone=(), payment=(), usb=()
